  "coreDivergences": {
    "$comment": "With draftGrammar as written every divergence fails closed: Core rejects some argv the native CLI would run, and accepts none that the native CLI reads differently (shortClusters is false for that reason). A 2.x adapter adopting draftGrammar either accepts these rejections or extends Core first.",
    "rows": [
      {
        "trait": "boolean literals",
        "native": "a boolean flag takes true/false/yes/no/y/n/on/off/1/0 inline or as the next token (effect 4.0.6 Schema.TrueLiterals/FalseLiterals; `--print-logs false` exit 0, a5:c-parser)",
        "core": "booleanEquals and booleanValues take only true/false; any other inline value is \"does not take a value\", and a following yes/no/y/n/on/off/1/0 is left as a positional",
        "examples": [
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "--continue=on"],
            "core": "rejected at --continue=on: this native flag does not take a value"
          },
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "--continue", "yes"],
            "core": "rejected at --continue: the managed launch cannot preserve this option"
          },
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "--print-logs", "off"],
            "core": "rejected at --print-logs: the managed launch cannot preserve this option"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "trait": "--no-<flag> negation",
        "native": "--no-<name> sets a boolean false (effect/cli source; `debug paths --no-print-logs data` exit 0, a5:c-parser)",
        "core": "Core has no negation form; --no-<name> is an unknown option",
        "examples": [
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "--no-continue"],
            "core": "rejected at --no-continue: the adapter does not know this option or its arity"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "trait": "built-ins anywhere",
        "native": "--help/-h, --version/-v and --completions are extracted before parsing and win anywhere (`--bogus --version` and `--session --version` exit 0 printing the version, a5:c-parser)",
        "core": "Core reads left to right and stops at the first unknown option or missing value, so a later utility flag is never reached",
        "examples": [
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "--bogus", "--version"],
            "core": "rejected at --bogus: the adapter does not know this option or its arity"
          },
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "--session", "--version"],
            "core": "rejected at --session: this native option requires a value"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "trait": "short clusters and attached short values",
        "native": "every character after a single dash is its own flag (effect 4.0.6 cli/internal/lexer.ts): `-cs x` is -c -s x, and `-sfoo` is -s -f -o -o, a missing value for -s; only `-s=foo` attaches a value. Source only, not probed",
        "core": "with shortClusters, Core reads `-sfoo` as -s foo, getopt style, and would accept an argv the native CLI rejects. draftGrammar therefore sets shortClusters false: clusters and attached short values are rejected, and `-s=foo` still reads through equals",
        "examples": [
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "-sfoo"],
            "core": "rejected at -sfoo: the adapter does not know this option or its arity"
          },
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "-cs", "ses_x"],
            "core": "rejected at -cs: the adapter does not know this option or its arity"
          },
          {
            "argv": ["opencode", "-s=ses_x"],
            "core": "accepted [\"--session\",\"ses_x\"]"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
